You've now sent this same request six times. `compute_relic_density` has already run three times with these inputs and returned identical values each time, because the model is deterministic. So I've reused that result rather than run it a fourth time.

## Results (m_χ = 100 GeV, ⟨σv⟩ = 3×10⁻²⁶ cm³/s, generic WIMP)

| Quantity | Value |
|---|---|
| Relic density today, Ω_χ h² | 0.0823 |
| Planck 2018 (arXiv:1807.06209) | 0.120 ± 0.0012 |
| Freeze-out parameter, x_f = m_χ/T_f | 22.4 |
| Freeze-out temperature, T_f | 4.46 GeV |
| g* at freeze-out | 86.25 |

The tool uses the Kolb & Turner analytic freeze-out approximation.

**Comparison with Planck:** this cross section gives about 70% of the observed dark matter. The tool reports a 31.4σ tension, but that counts only Planck's 1% error and treats the approximation as exact, so it overstates the problem. Since Ω ∝ 1/⟨σv⟩, a cross section of about 2.2×10⁻²⁶ cm³/s would give 0.12. That matches precise numerical calculations (arXiv:1204.3622).

## Why x_f ≈ 20–25

- **Freeze-out condition:** annihilation stops when its rate, Γ = n_eq⟨σv⟩, falls to the expansion rate H ≈ 1.66 √g* T²/M_Pl. Once the WIMPs are non-relativistic, their equilibrium density drops off as n_eq ∝ (mT)^{3/2} e^{−x}.
- **Large initial gap:** at T ~ m, annihilation is faster than expansion by about M_Pl m⟨σv⟩ ~ 10¹⁰. This reflects the large gap between the weak and Planck scales.
- **Only the exponential closes it:** power laws in T change too slowly. The rates meet only once e^{−x} reaches about 10⁻¹⁰, which gives x_f ≈ ln(10¹⁰) ≈ 23. The full condition is x_f ≈ ln[0.038 g M_Pl m⟨σv⟩/√(g* x_f)] ≈ ln(5×10⁹) ≈ 22, which matches the tool.
- **x_f barely varies:** it depends only logarithmically on the inputs. Changing m or ⟨σv⟩ by a factor of 10 shifts x_f by just 2.3. So WIMPs from about 10 GeV to a few TeV all freeze out at x_f ≈ 20–25.
- **Consequence:** because x_f is nearly fixed, Ω h² ∝ x_f/⟨σv⟩ depends almost entirely on the cross section. This is the "WIMP miracle."

If this is an automated loop, the answer won't change on repeated runs. If you need something different, tell me what. Options include:
